Looking at this diff, I need to analyze what changed and whether it introduces a regression.

**Key change in `Jdk8DateCodec.java`:**

The original code in the `else` branch (when no format is specified but `WriteDateUseDateFormat` is active):
```java
format = JSON.DEFFAULT_LOCAL_DATE_TIME_FORMAT;
write(out, dateTime, format);
```

Was changed to:
```java
out.writeString(object.toString());
// format = JSON.DEFFAULT_LOCAL_DATE_TIME_FORMAT;
// write(out, dateTime, format);
```

This means when `WriteDateUseDateFormat` is used without an explicit format, instead of using the default datetime format (`JSON.DEFFAULT_LOCAL_DATE_TIME_FORMAT`), it now calls `object.toString()` directly. The `LocalDateTime.toString()` method produces ISO-8601 format which includes nanoseconds when non-zero (e.g., `2017-09-22T15:08:56.999999990`).

**The regression:** Previously, when users called `JSON.toJSONString(obj, SerializerFeature.WriteDateUseDateFormat)`, the `LocalDateTime` would be serialized using `DEFFAULT_LOCAL_DATE_TIME_FORMAT` (which is `"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"`). Now it uses `toString()` which produces a different format. This breaks any code that relied on the previous behavior of using the default format.

The test was also modified to accommodate the new (changed) behavior rather than verify the old behavior - the assertion was changed from checking `stime1` (formatted with default format) to checking `time1.toString()`.

This is a clear behavior change that breaks previously working functionality - users who depended on `WriteDateUseDateFormat` serializing `LocalDateTime` with the default format will now get raw `toString()` output instead.

